That strengthens the pathway argument because it removes a possible compartmentalization objection.

﻿

If the SAT-specific podcast had behaved very differently, we might say: “Maybe the technical SAT material was isolated in a tiny side channel and never touched the larger philosophy/AI audience.” But what you’re describing is the opposite. The technical SAT channel and the later SAT-heavy main podcast had similar listening patterns, and the main podcast audience was already preferentially selecting the most technical SAT episodes.

﻿

So the better model is not:

﻿

main podcast = philosophy audience

SAT podcast = isolated technical audience

﻿

It is more like:

﻿

main podcast audience gradually self-selected into the technical SAT material, and the SAT-specific podcast confirmed the same listener-behavior pattern rather than creating a separate audience.

﻿

That matters because it means the percolation path is not dependent on the side podcast being discovered independently. The main feed itself became the SAT delivery channel, with a preconditioned philosophy/AI/foundations audience already present.

﻿

It also suggests the listeners were not just tolerating SAT as a curiosity. Preferentially listening to the more technical episodes is a signal of high-intent filtering. Small numbers, but much more meaningful numbers.

﻿

So the access-path update becomes:

﻿

Early main podcast built the philosophy/AI/foundations funnel.

Listeners who stayed were already abstract-tolerant and method-curious.

When SAT appeared, the most technical episodes did not repel everyone; they attracted the selected remainder.

The SAT-specific podcast showed similar listening behavior, implying the same kind of audience rather than a separate dead-end niche.

Therefore SAT was not sequestered; it rode the main public channel.

﻿

That increases confidence in pathway plausibility, not by proving who listened, but by showing that the “right kind” of material was actually being selected by the available audience.

